//json web token
 Step 1 — Install

npm install jsonwebtoken
Add to .env:


JWT_SECRET=some_long_random_string
Why a secret? The server signs tokens with this secret. Only someone who knows the secret can produce a valid token. If you leak it, anyone can forge a token for any user — so keep it in .env, never in code.


After you've verified the user's password is correct:


import jwt from "jsonwebtoken";

const token = jwt.sign(
  { id: user._id, email: user.email },   // payload
  process.env.JWT_SECRET,                // secret
  { expiresIn: "7d" }                    // options
);
What's happening:

jwt.sign(...) takes your payload, base64-encodes it, then signs it with the secret → produces the xxx.yyy.zzz string.
Payload — put only identifying info (user id, email). Never put the password. The payload is not encrypted, just encoded — anyone can decode and read it. The signature only prevents tampering, not reading.
expiresIn: "7d" — token becomes invalid after 7 days. Forces re-login, limits damage if stolen.



